    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white text-center">Consent vs Waiver: What Your Form Must Actually Prove</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">Most studios use "consent form," "waiver," and "release" interchangeably, but they serve different jobs. <strong class="text-white">Informed consent</strong> documents that the client understands the procedure is permanent, was told the risks (infection, allergic reaction, scarring), and agreed freely — this is your defense against a negligence claim. The <strong class="text-white">liability waiver / release</strong> limits the studio's exposure. A proper form includes both, plus a health-screening block and an aftercare acknowledgment.</p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">The minor question is where most shops get burned. Rules are set state-by-state: Florida requires notarized parental consent for ages 16–17; Texas requires in-person notarized parental consent; California and New York ban tattooing minors entirely, even with parental permission. A generic template that doesn't account for your state can be invalid on its face. Verify your statute — and have an attorney review your final form.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

